{"st":"MN","name":"Minnesota","seats":[{"census":1850,"seats":2},{"census":1860,"seats":2},{"census":1870,"seats":3},{"census":1880,"seats":5},{"census":1890,"seats":7},{"census":1900,"seats":9},{"census":1910,"seats":10},{"census":1920,"seats":10},{"census":1930,"seats":9},{"census":1940,"seats":9},{"census":1950,"seats":9},{"census":1960,"seats":8},{"census":1970,"seats":8},{"census":1980,"seats":8},{"census":1990,"seats":8},{"census":2000,"seats":8},{"census":2010,"seats":8},{"census":2020,"seats":8}],"events":[{"congress":35,"year":1856,"kind":"at-large","title":"Statewide (general ticket) elections","text":"In the 35th through 37th Congresses (1857–1863), Minnesota elected all of its representatives statewide on a general ticket instead of by district.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/"]},{"congress":35,"year":1858,"kind":"statehood","title":"Statehood","text":"Minnesota became the 32nd state on May 11, 1858. Congress gave it 2 seats in the House until the next apportionment.","sources":["https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/R47747.html","https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":38,"year":1862,"kind":"census","title":"1860 census apportionment","text":"After the 1860 census, Minnesota kept 2 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":43,"year":1872,"kind":"census","title":"1870 census apportionment","text":"After the 1870 census, Minnesota's House delegation grew from 2 to 3 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":48,"year":1882,"kind":"census","title":"1880 census apportionment","text":"After the 1880 census, Minnesota's House delegation grew from 3 to 5 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":53,"year":1892,"kind":"census","title":"1890 census apportionment","text":"After the 1890 census, Minnesota's House delegation grew from 5 to 7 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":58,"year":1902,"kind":"census","title":"1900 census apportionment","text":"After the 1900 census, Minnesota's House delegation grew from 7 to 9 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":63,"year":1912,"kind":"census","title":"1910 census apportionment","text":"After the 1910 census, Minnesota's House delegation grew from 9 to 10 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":63,"year":1912,"kind":"at-large","title":"Some members elected at-large","text":"In the 63rd Congress (1913–1915), Minnesota elected one of its representatives statewide (at-large) and the rest by district.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/"]},{"congress":72,"year":1932,"kind":"court","title":"Smiley v. Holm","text":"The Supreme Court held that a governor's veto applies to congressional maps passed by a legislature. Minnesota had no valid map, so it elected all nine House members statewide in 1932.","sources":["https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/285/355","https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators"]},{"congress":73,"year":1932,"kind":"census","title":"1930 census apportionment","text":"After the 1930 census, Minnesota's House delegation shrank from 10 to 9 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":73,"year":1932,"kind":"at-large","title":"Statewide (general ticket) elections","text":"In the 73rd Congress (1933–1935), Minnesota elected all of its representatives statewide on a general ticket instead of by district. The 1929 apportionment law had no districting rules, so states without a new map after losing or gaining seats could elect members at-large.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/","https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/IF12567.html"]},{"congress":78,"year":1942,"kind":"census","title":"1940 census apportionment","text":"After the 1940 census, Minnesota kept 9 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":83,"year":1952,"kind":"census","title":"1950 census apportionment","text":"After the 1950 census, Minnesota kept 9 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":88,"year":1962,"kind":"census","title":"1960 census apportionment","text":"After the 1960 census, Minnesota's House delegation shrank from 9 to 8 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":93,"year":1972,"kind":"census","title":"1970 census apportionment","text":"After the 1970 census, Minnesota kept 8 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":98,"year":1982,"kind":"census","title":"1980 census apportionment","text":"After the 1980 census, Minnesota kept 8 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":103,"year"